<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="JirAI: Sign in and manage your account" id="JirAI-Sign-In-and-Configuration">

    <primary-label ref="jirai"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Sign in: <control>JirAI</control> tool window, <control>Log In</control></p>
        <p>Manage the account: <ui-path>Settings | Tools | JirAI</ui-path></p>
        <p>Requires: a Jira Cloud site and an Atlassian API token</p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Sign in to Jira Cloud with an API token, fix sign-in errors, manage or switch the account and use JirAI behind a proxy.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Sign in with an Atlassian API token, fix sign-in errors, switch sites and see where JirAI stores credentials.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Sign in to Jira Cloud from JirAI in JetBrains IDEs with an Atlassian API token, fix sign-in errors, replace an expired token and use JirAI behind a corporate proxy.</web-summary>

    <p>JirAI connects to one Jira Cloud site with your Atlassian account e-mail and an API token.</p>

    <chapter title="Sign in from the tool window" id="sign-in">
        <procedure title="Sign in to Jira Cloud" id="sign-in-proc">
            <p>Before you start, create an API token with <control>Create API token</control> on the <a href="https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens">Atlassian API tokens page</a>.</p>
            <p>Do not use <control>Create API token with scopes</control>: JirAI cannot sign in with a scoped token.</p>
            <step>
                <p>Open the <control>JirAI</control> tool window on the left side of the IDE.</p>
                <p>When you are not signed in, it shows <control>Connect to Jira</control>.</p>
                <!-- planned-image src="jirai_connect_panel.png" alt="JirAI tool window asking you to connect to Jira, with the Log In button" -->
            </step>
            <step><p>Click <control>Log In</control>.</p></step>
            <step>
                <p>In the <control>Log in to Jira</control> dialog, fill in the fields:</p>
                <deflist type="medium">
                    <def title="Server">
                        <p>Your Jira Cloud site, for example <code>demo-org.atlassian.net</code>.</p>
                        <p>A bare host name is enough, and an <code>https://</code> address copied from the browser also works.</p>
                        <p>JirAI refuses <code>http://</code> addresses, because the token would travel unencrypted.</p>
                    </def>
                    <def title="Email">The e-mail address of the Atlassian account that owns the API token.</def>
                    <def title="Token">The API token. The <control>Generate an API token</control> link below the field opens the Atlassian page where you create one.</def>
                </deflist>
                <!-- planned-image src="jirai_login_dialog.png" alt="Log in to Jira dialog with the Server, Email and Token fields" -->
            </step>
            <step><p>Click <control>Log In</control>.</p></step>
            <p>JirAI checks the credentials with Jira before it stores anything.</p>
            <p>When the check succeeds, the tool window lists the issues assigned to you.</p>
            <p>To list other issues, see <a href="Work-with-Jira-Issues.topic" anchor="filters">Filter the list</a>.</p>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="If sign-in fails" id="sign-in-errors">
        <p>The <control>Log in to Jira</control> dialog shows why the sign-in failed:</p>
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="Jira rejected those credentials. Check the email address, and that the API token is current.">
                <p>Check that <control>Email</control> is the address of the Atlassian account that owns the token.</p>
                <p>Check that the token has not expired or been revoked, and that it was created without scopes.</p>
                <p>If in doubt, create a new token.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="Those credentials are valid but do not have access to this site.">
                <p>Ask the site administrator for access, or sign in to a site your account can use.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="No Jira site responded at that address, or it is not visible to you.">
                <p>Check the spelling of <control>Server</control>, for example <code>demo-org.atlassian.net</code>.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="Could not reach Jira. Check your network connection.">
                <p>Check the spelling of <control>Server</control>, your network connection and the IDE proxy settings.</p>
                <p>See <a anchor="corporate-proxy">Use JirAI behind a corporate proxy</a>.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="Could not reach Jira: the HTTP proxy requires credentials. This is not a problem with your Jira token.">
                <p>Enter or correct the proxy password in the IDE's <control>HTTP Proxy</control> settings, then try again.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="Jira is rate-limiting requests right now. Wait a moment and try again.">
                <p>Wait a minute, then click <control>Log In</control> again.</p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Manage the account in Settings" id="settings-account">
        <p>You can also sign in and sign out without opening the tool window.</p>
        <procedure title="Sign in or out from Settings" id="settings-account-proc">
            <step><p>Open <ui-path>Settings | Tools | JirAI</ui-path>.</p></step>
            <step>
                <p>In the <control>Account</control> group, do one of the following:</p>
                <list>
                    <li><p>To sign in, click <control>+</control> and complete the <control>Log in to Jira</control> dialog.</p></li>
                    <li><p>To sign out, select the account, click <control>−</control> and confirm.</p></li>
                </list>
                <!-- planned-image src="jirai_settings_account.png" alt="Settings page Tools JirAI with the signed-in account in the Account group" -->
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>The <control>Resources</control> group on the same page links to this documentation.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="See the connected site and sign out" id="account-management">
        <p>The tool window's options menu (<control>⋮</control>) shows the connected site and the account you are signed in as, for example <control>demo-org.atlassian.net — Signed in as Alex Doe</control>.</p>
        <procedure title="Sign out from the tool window" id="sign-out-proc">
            <step>
                <p>In the <control>JirAI</control> tool window, click the options menu (<control>⋮</control>).</p>
                <!-- planned-image src="jirai_gear_menu.png" alt="JirAI tool window options menu with the connected site and Sign Out" -->
            </step>
            <step><p>Choose <control>Sign Out</control>.</p></step>
        </procedure>
        <p>Signing out removes the stored API token.</p>
        <p>It keeps your hidden fields, Doc Push targets and house standard, which apply again when you sign in to the same site.</p>
        <p>JirAI connects to one site at a time.</p>
        <procedure title="Switch sites or replace the API token" id="switch-site-or-token">
            <p>Use this procedure to connect to a different Jira Cloud site, or when your API token expired or was revoked.</p>
            <p>An Atlassian API token expires on the date chosen when it was created, at most one year later.</p>
            <p>If you need a new token, create it first on the <a href="https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens">Atlassian API tokens page</a>.</p>
            <step>
                <p>In the <control>JirAI</control> tool window, click the options menu (<control>⋮</control>) and choose <control>Sign Out</control>.</p>
                <p>Alternatively, select the account in <ui-path>Settings | Tools | JirAI</ui-path>, click <control>−</control> and confirm.</p>
            </step>
            <step><p>In the tool window, click <control>Log In</control>.</p></step>
            <step>
                <p>In the <control>Log in to Jira</control> dialog, check <control>Server</control> and <control>Email</control>, which JirAI fills in from your last sign-in.</p>
                <p>If you switch sites, change them.</p>
            </step>
            <step><p>Paste the API token into <control>Token</control> and click <control>Log In</control>.</p></step>
        </procedure>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where JirAI stores credentials" id="credential-storage">
        <list>
            <li>
                <p>The API token is stored in the IDE credential store, which is the operating system keychain or the IDE's KeePass file, depending on the IDE's <control>Passwords</control> setting.</p>
                <p>The token is never written to an XML file.</p>
            </li>
            <li><p>The site address, your e-mail, display name and account ID are stored without secrets in <path>jira-sites.xml</path> in the IDE configuration directory.</p></li>
            <li><p>Under Remote Development, the token and <path>jira-sites.xml</path> stay on the host.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Use JirAI behind a corporate proxy" id="corporate-proxy">
        <p>JirAI uses the IDE proxy settings in <ui-path>Settings | Appearance &amp; Behavior | System Settings | HTTP Proxy</ui-path>.</p>
        <list>
            <li><p>Under Remote Development, requests run on the host, so the host's proxy settings apply.</p></li>
            <li><p>For an authenticating proxy, JirAI uses the proxy credentials the IDE already has and does not open its own password prompt.</p></li>
            <li><p>When the proxy rejects the credentials, the error names the proxy rather than Jira.</p></li>
            <li><p>JirAI does not support SOCKS proxies: when the IDE is set to a SOCKS proxy, JirAI connects to Jira directly, without the proxy, which fails on a network that allows only proxied traffic.</p></li>
            <li><p>Behind an authenticating proxy, user avatars might not load, because they are fetched without credentials.</p></li>
        </list>
        <p>If sign-in through a proxy fails, enter or correct the proxy password in the IDE's <control>HTTP Proxy</control> settings and try again.</p>
    </chapter>

